{:edb/db-before-t 2777 :edb/db-after-t 2778 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536090 50 #inst "2026-10-09T14:46:10.905Z" 13194139536090 true] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383161 13194139536090 true] [17592186221346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383168 13194139536090 true] [17592186221346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383175 13194139536090 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383182 13194139536090 true] [17592186221936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536090 true] [17592186221936 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139536090 true] [17592186221936 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $λ = 0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v = [- 1, 1]^{T}$. The vector $w = [1, 0]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139536090 false] [17592186221936 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/9988933a3f4393f745b1df6f0dc667a4074f5ee787fab43fb392b117368a8ca9.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/2809f28b49c9782db6fdf3f5a8be7fd99ec6d9a08db8e81d91c4d6bc53efcdda.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7fd67e8a768419dd497bc6345c5999ff921e6de492f60c5f217e93f8dc4d07c1.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png)" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383162 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1089 "q-333801" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1113 #uuid "315ce7db-c9bd-5e7b-89ff-9a198ba7f9b9" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383161 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383163 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383164 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383165 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383166 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383167 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1041 "selection" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383166 13194139536090 true] [17592186383162 1110 #uuid "4ed1c000-f5a1-5681-9f84-e7ad7ca846cb" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383163 1034 "y=\\frac{4}{3}x" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536090 true] [17592186383163 1111 #uuid "aaef20bd-2e1c-5084-83b3-6cb7de2c19e5" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383164 1034 "y=-\\frac{4}{3}x" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536090 true] [17592186383164 1111 #uuid "7ae8e92c-f3c7-566e-95c7-e2af74b64177" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383165 1034 "y=\\frac{3}{4}x-1" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536090 true] [17592186383165 1111 #uuid "1ada8cd6-adb7-5b84-8f4b-f36d29d928e1" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383166 1034 "y=\\frac{3}{4}x" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536090 true] [17592186383166 1111 #uuid "ca8cb3b9-d036-58cb-9784-4aafd495927f" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383167 1034 "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536090 true] [17592186383167 1111 #uuid "777d3f1f-6936-5e4e-994b-118b3c884a04" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383169 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1089 "q-333824" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1113 #uuid "5ec66512-f01a-54c5-9b43-60952356c9b6" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383168 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383170 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383171 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383172 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383173 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383174 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1041 "selection" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383172 13194139536090 true] [17592186383169 1110 #uuid "bf7e486d-a89d-5302-baff-701223780b7a" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383170 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536090 true] [17592186383170 1111 #uuid "71bc4467-5fee-5edc-81ea-6f6e7b365273" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383171 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536090 true] [17592186383171 1111 #uuid "6e8dc7f9-9328-5356-9480-e8a45926bc09" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383172 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536090 true] [17592186383172 1111 #uuid "c445bdb2-5211-5e50-af7e-091ca08706bf" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383173 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536090 true] [17592186383173 1111 #uuid "e0e1c109-b971-597a-b97d-4ddd2e2b0720" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383174 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png" 13194139536090 true] [17592186383174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536090 true] [17592186383174 1111 #uuid "b95654cf-68b4-5ccb-8766-2050